In order for us to "make order of the chaos" that is tcpdump stdout - we must integrate several common flags,
filters and operators. This guide will cover the most important keywords for our honeypot,
as well as several dedicated examples.

## Must-Have Flags ##

-q = Quiet
-n = Skip dns resolution (means you get the direct IP)
-tttt = Set timestamps 
-v = Verbose output [avoid if you don't want to flood your logs]
-i <int> = interface
-r <file> = Read packets from a pcap file

## Recommended Filters ##

ip = IPv4 ONLY
not port <your_ssh_port> — Obviously you want to exclude yourself from the captured traffic...
not arp — remove address resolution from routers
src & dst — trusted hosts / nets to or from whom you create traffic, it comes in handy to filter them out
tcp / udp / icmp — protocol

## Examples ##

- Write and read output to a file:
sudo tcpdump -tttt -l -q -n not port <ssh_port> and not arp and not <trusted_host> and ip > example_one.txt
sudo tcpdump -r example_one.txt
(If that won't open as hooman user - try "$ sudo -su tcpdump" and open it from there!)

- Filter internal routers ipv6 traffic
sudo tcpdump -tttt -l -q -n not port <ssh_port> and not net fe80::/16

- Filter directionally
sudo tcpdump -tttt -l -q -v not port <ssh_port> and not dst <trusted_dst> and not src <trusted_src> and ip

- To monitor what a scanner is hitting, combine with grep:
sudo tcpdump -i eth0 -nn -A 'host 45.33.14.197' | grep -iE 'GET|POST|Host:'
